\chapter{When the model will not fit}

Mixed models fail to converge more often than any other model in this book. This
chapter is a field guide to the warnings and what to do about each one.

\section{``boundary (singular) fit''}

<<singular, warning=TRUE, message=TRUE>>=
set.seed(15)
d <- data.frame(g = factor(rep(1:8, each = 6)), x = rnorm(48))
d$y <- 2 + 0.6 * d$x + rnorm(48)          # NO group effect at all
m <- lme4::lmer(y ~ x + (1 | g), data = d)
lme4::VarCorr(m)
lme4::isSingular(m)
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What it means]
A variance component was estimated as zero (or a correlation as exactly $\pm 1$).
The model is sitting on the edge of its parameter space. The estimate is not
\emph{wrong}, but the model is more complex than the data can support.
\end{plainenglish}

Common causes and their fixes:

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.54\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Cause} & \textbf{Fix} \\
\midrule
There genuinely is no group-level variation & Drop the random effect; report why \\
Too few groups (fewer than about 5) & Fit the grouping as a fixed effect instead \\
Random slope not supported by the data & Simplify: \fn{(x|g)} $\to$ \fn{(1|g)} \\
Correlation between intercept and slope near $\pm 1$ & Use \fn{(x||g)} to force independence \\
Too many random terms & Remove them one at a time, comparing with LRT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

<<singular-fix>>=
m_simple <- lm(y ~ x, data = d)
round(c(lmer_slope = lme4::fixef(m)["x"], lm_slope = coef(m_simple)["x"]), 4)
@

With a zero variance component, the mixed model has collapsed to the ordinary
linear model anyway --- which is why the estimates match exactly.

\section{``Model failed to converge with max|grad| \dots''}

This says the optimiser stopped somewhere the gradient is not quite zero. Sometimes
it is a real failure; often it is a false alarm from a conservative check.

\subsection{Step 1: scale your predictors}

By far the most common cause, and the easiest fix. Optimisers struggle when
predictors are on wildly different scales.

<<scaling, warning=TRUE, message=TRUE, size='scriptsize'>>=
set.seed(6)
n  <- 600
dd <- data.frame(
  g      = factor(rep(1:30, each = 20)),
  income = rnorm(n, 50000, 15000),        # ~10^4
  age    = rnorm(n, 40, 12)               # ~10^1
)
dd$y <- rbinom(n, 1, plogis(-3 + 0.00002 * dd$income + 0.02 * dd$age +
                           rep(rnorm(30, 0, 0.5), each = 20)))

## Unscaled: the two predictors differ by four orders of magnitude
bad <- lme4::glmer(y ~ income + age + (1 | g), family = binomial, data = dd)

## Scaled: same model, well-conditioned
dd$income_s <- scale(dd$income); dd$age_s <- scale(dd$age)
good <- lme4::glmer(y ~ income_s + age_s + (1 | g), family = binomial, data = dd)
round(c(logLik_unscaled = logLik(bad), logLik_scaled = logLik(good)), 4)
@

The two models are statistically identical, but the scaled version is far easier to
optimise. Rescale coefficients afterwards by dividing by the standard deviation of
the original variable.

\begin{rulebox}
Centre and scale every continuous predictor before fitting a GLMM. It costs one
line, it never hurts, and it prevents most convergence warnings.
\end{rulebox}

\subsection{Step 2: try other optimisers}

If several independent optimisers land in the same place, the warning was a false
alarm.

<<allfit, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
af <- lme4::allFit(good, verbose = FALSE)
ll <- sort(sapply(af[!sapply(af, is, "error")], logLik), decreasing = TRUE)
round(ll, 5)
round(diff(range(ll)), 8)          # spread across optimisers
@

All optimisers agree to
\Sexpr{signif(diff(range(ll)), 2)} of a log-likelihood unit. That is convergence,
whatever the warning said.

<<optimizer-control, eval=FALSE>>=
## Change the optimiser directly
lme4::glmer(y ~ income_s + age_s + (1 | g), family = binomial, data = dd,
            control = lme4::glmerControl(optimizer = "bobyqa",
                                         optCtrl = list(maxfun = 2e5)))
@

\subsection{Step 3: increase the accuracy of the likelihood}

For binomial GLMMs with a single random effect, \fn{nAGQ} controls how accurately
the random effects are integrated out. The default (\fn{nAGQ = 1}, the Laplace
approximation) can be poor with small clusters or rare events.

<<nagq>>=
data(cbpp, package = "lme4")
f <- cbind(incidence, size - incidence) ~ period + (1 | herd)
round(sapply(c(1, 5, 15), function(q)
  lme4::fixef(lme4::glmer(f, family = binomial, data = cbpp, nAGQ = q))), 4)
@

The estimates barely move, so the Laplace approximation was fine here. If they
\emph{do} move noticeably, use the higher \fn{nAGQ} value.

\begin{warningbox}[\fn{nAGQ > 1} has limits]
It only works with a \emph{single}, scalar random effect. With crossed random
effects or random slopes you are restricted to \fn{nAGQ = 1}. If accuracy is a
concern there, switch to \pkg{glmmTMB}, which uses a different and generally more
accurate approximation, or go Bayesian with \pkg{brms}.
\end{warningbox}

\subsection{Step 4: simplify the random structure}

The most reliable fix of all. Random effects are expensive: \fn{(1 + x + z | g)}
estimates six parameters (three variances and three correlations) from however many
groups you have.

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.34\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.32\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Random structure} & \textbf{Parameters} & \textbf{Groups you need} \\
\midrule
\fn{(1 | g)}          & 1  & 5+ (ideally 20+) \\
\fn{(x || g)}         & 2  & 20+ \\
\fn{(x | g)}          & 3  & 30+ \\
\fn{(x + z | g)}      & 6  & 50+ \\
\fn{(x * z | g)}      & 10 & rarely justified \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{rulebox}[The order to remove things]
1. Correlations between random terms (\fn{|} $\to$ \fn{||}). \
2. Random slopes for interactions. \
3. Random slopes for predictors with little within-group variation. \
4. Whole random effects with tiny estimated variances. \
Never remove a random effect that reflects the actual design of your study just
because it is inconvenient --- if pupils are in schools, that structure is real.
\end{rulebox}

\section{``fixed-effect model matrix is rank deficient''}

Two of your predictors carry the same information --- perfectly collinear, or a
factor level that never occurs in the data.

<<rank-deficient, warning=TRUE, message=TRUE>>=
dz <- data.frame(x = rnorm(50)); dz$x2 <- 2 * dz$x        # exact duplicate
dz$y  <- rpois(50, exp(1 + 0.4 * dz$x))
mz <- glm(y ~ x + x2, family = poisson, data = dz)
coef(mz)
@

\R{} drops the redundant column and returns \fn{NA}. Find the culprit before
proceeding:

<<collinearity>>=
## Which factor combinations never occur?
data(quine, package = "MASS")
table(quine$Age, quine$Lrn)
## Variance inflation factors for continuous predictors
car::vif(glm(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = poisson, data = quine))
@

A GVIF (last column, squared and scaled) above about 2--3 is worth investigating;
above 5 usually means two predictors are measuring the same thing.

Notice also that \fn{Age = F3} never occurs with \fn{Lrn = SL}. Any model with an
\fn{Age:Lrn} interaction would be rank deficient for exactly that reason.

\section{glmmTMB warnings}

\pkg{glmmTMB} reports problems differently.

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.46\textwidth}p{0.50\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Message} & \textbf{Meaning and fix} \\
\midrule
\fn{NA/NaN function evaluation} & Usually harmless --- the optimiser probed an
  illegal region and backed out. Check the fit converged. \\
\fn{Model convergence problem; non-positive-definite Hessian} & Serious. A
  parameter is unidentified. Simplify the model. \\
\fn{Std. Errors are NaN} & Same cause: the model is over-parameterised. \\
Variance estimate at zero & Same as a singular fit in \pkg{lme4}. Drop that term. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

<<glmmtmb-diag>>=
library(glmmTMB)
data(grouseticks, package = "lme4")
grouseticks$cH <- scale(grouseticks$HEIGHT)
gt <- glmmTMB(TICKS ~ cH + YEAR + (1 | BROOD), family = nbinom2, data = grouseticks)
gt$fit$convergence            # 0 means the optimiser converged
gt$sdr$pdHess                 # TRUE means the Hessian is well behaved
@

\section{A triage flowchart}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily\footnotesize, node distance=5mm,
  b/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=rule1, fill=white, align=left,
            inner sep=5pt, text width=104mm},
  ar/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, draw=rule1, thick}]
\node[b, fill=primarylt, draw=primary] (n1)
  {\textbf{1.} Centre and scale every continuous predictor. Re-fit.};
\node[b, below=of n1] (n2)
  {\textbf{2.} Check for singular fit: \fn{isSingular(m)}. If TRUE, simplify the
   random structure.};
\node[b, below=of n2] (n3)
  {\textbf{3.} Run \fn{allFit()}. If all optimisers agree, the warning was a false
   alarm --- proceed.};
\node[b, below=of n3] (n4)
  {\textbf{4.} Try \fn{bobyqa} or \fn{Nelder\_Mead} with a higher \fn{maxfun}.};
\node[b, below=of n4] (n5)
  {\textbf{5.} Simplify: correlations first, then random slopes, then whole terms.};
\node[b, below=of n5] (n6)
  {\textbf{6.} Switch package: \fn{glmmTMB} often fits what \fn{glmer} cannot.};
\node[b, below=of n6, fill=greenlt, draw=greenbox] (n7)
  {\textbf{7.} Still failing? Go Bayesian (\pkg{brms}, \pkg{rstanarm}) --- weak
   priors regularise the variance components and the model will fit.};
\foreach \a/\b in {n1/n2, n2/n3, n3/n4, n4/n5, n5/n6, n6/n7} \draw[ar] (\a) -- (\b);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\section{Warnings you can safely ignore}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \fn{non-integer \#successes in a binomial glm!} --- expected when you use
        \fn{weights} with proportions.
  \item \fn{non-integer x = \dots} from a quasi-Poisson on continuous data ---
        expected, and harmless, because quasi-likelihood does not require integers.
  \item \fn{max|grad| = 0.0015} where \fn{allFit} shows all optimisers agreeing to
        many decimal places.
  \item \fn{fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1} \emph{if} you have checked
        there is no separation and the coefficients are sensible.
\end{itemize}

\begin{warningbox}[Warnings you must never ignore]
Coefficients in the hundreds. Standard errors of \fn{NaN} or in the thousands. A
non-positive-definite Hessian. A variance component of exactly zero that your
design says should not be zero. Each of these means a number in your output is not
what it claims to be.
\end{warningbox}
